A||||16||Homicidal||I own an 84', chances of it catching fire?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||08-10-2000||04:14 AM||||All this chat about the fire issue have me concernced.  How can I basically insure that my fiero won't be a marshmellow anytime soon (if it's possible)?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-10-2000||09:31 AM||||Keep the engine compartment clean.  No oil leaks, no rubbish collection (leaves etc in the engine compartment)||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||08-10-2000||09:48 AM||||Two rumors I've heard explaining the 84 fires were improperly installed fuel filters and spontaneous combustion due to excessively low oil levels.<P>I can't say whether these are true or not - but I always kept the oil in my 84 topped off and made sure the fuel filter fittings were snug (not overtight) and installed in the correct flow direction!||||reg||4
Z||000003||deceler8||08-10-2000||09:55 AM||||It's entirely possible the engine (or a good part of it) has been replaced in your '84.<P>At the very least, it was torn down under recall and all your connecting rods, etc. have been replaced. Your car should also be using a larger oil filter, not the puny PH3387A or whatever it was originally.<P>The advice about keeping the engine bay clean is very important. Keep an eye on valve cover grommets and the valve cover itself for leakage. Using a part synthetic oil can reduce engine oil temp, as can a 180 degree thermostat.<P>And carry a fire extinguisher with you...I do and always have. I've never used it on any of my cars, but have put out someone else's fires on occasion.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||Homicidal||08-10-2000||12:59 PM||||Umm, I know my engine compartment is.........less than par for cleanliness.  But I am DEATHLY afraid to touch the engine, since I am not familiar with cars.  It's really dirty, and probably has tons of oil leaks.  But I heard if you clean up an engine you can expose new leaks that the old oil has clogged up!||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||08-10-2000||01:19 PM||||   Many Fiero fires were caused by engine oil  leaking and dripping onto the catalytic converter.  Heres what you do:<P>   Buy a can of engine degreaser, spray the whole can on the engine and engine bay and let it soak for 20 min.  THen rinse it off under pressure at a carwash.  To do this you'll want the engine warm but not hot.  Let the exhaust manifold cool off before spraying it with water.<P>  This should clean most of the grease off and won't cause any new leaks.  It will just be easier to spot where your old leaks are and fix them.  A can of degreaser and a gasket or 2 is a small price to pay to keep your Fiero from catching fire.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||08-10-2000||01:21 PM||||   Never heard that it will unclog your existing leaks, you may notice where your leaks are coming from easier.  A clean engine will run cooler too.  I had a leaky valve cover (cheap silicone) gasket when got my car and noticed after driving the car a long way the oil puddled on the head just above the exhaust header     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  I'm wondering if that's one of the infamous causes of fire!<P>  I replaced it with a good neoprene one, and in a hour or two fixed the leak.  Whether you do it on your own or have a shop do it, it's something that you should try to fix if it's leaking.  Having a new valve cover gasket put on shouldn't set you back too far anyway.  Hope this helps...<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">     <BR>     <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 08-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||08-10-2000||05:21 PM||||If it's a 4 spd, Yes it will brst into flames  if you don't send it to me immediatly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Recall work was done to all 84's so it shouldn't have anymore of a chance of burning  than any other year.<P>Then again, I don't know how many people would try to firebomb your car and make it look look like an accident.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||08-10-2000||07:27 PM||||Since your car is fairly low miles, I would change to full synthetic oil, Mobil One is all I use. I broke a rod,sending it out the side of the block right above the pan and out the bottom of my pan. My muffler caught 4 quarts of hot Mobil One and while it smoked heavily, did not ignite,<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Homicidal||08-10-2000||07:36 PM||||A bit more expensive for full synthetic?||||reg||1
Z||000010||My7Fieros||08-10-2000||08:53 PM||||Not all the 84s had the recall done. Only the ones in which people took their cars to have it done. Id check that first, if it hasnt been done..GET IT DONE!! They should still do it for free. <BR>The fires were caused by crappy rods that would break sending them through the block..oil dumps onto the exhaust manifold or cat converter...boom, flames. <BR>Call 1-800-PMCARES to see if its been done.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||08-10-2000||09:17 PM||||Last time I brought this up it turned into a massive flame battle (pun intended) but I always take my car ro Walmart for oil changes. They charge like $22 for standard Pennzoil change. For $5 more they will put in any type of oil requested. Do the math- about $4.50/qt for Mobil1. Oil filter $2-3. You are now paying someone else $7 to get dirty and keep your driveway oilstain fee. It's a bargain for me.  Just make sure you check the work before you dive off. These guys get paid $6 an hour. They are NOT mechancs.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||DjKid||08-12-2000||01:01 AM||||19.95$ for a Qstate oil change at WallyWorld I worked at... 2$ more for regular oil other than qstate.. 5$ more for semisynthetic and 10$ more for full... thats a full 15point change.. I hated doing 15point changes.. took too long.. But I wouldn't let em do my oil... I dun trust anyone but me doing my Fiero.. heh..<P>------------------<BR>DjKid - 88 Coupe<BR>'Blue Knight"||||reg||1
Z||000013||Homicidal||08-12-2000||04:42 AM||||I took my car into a Pontiac Dealer to get a belt changed a while ago, I'm SURE they would have said something! Right?||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GT||08-12-2000||09:30 AM||||I agree with everyone else, keep the engine bay as clean as possible and go get you a fire extinguisher.  I have one and it fits nicely behind the passenger seat.  You never know when you could need it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Old Lar||08-12-2000||09:35 AM||||Mr Goodwrench may have a credibility problem suggestion additional work.  You may be thinking they just want to make more money and are recommending unneccessary stuff to jack up the charges.  <P>I requested to have all my belts and hoses changed after 9 years and the dealer whined. "Its not necessary" Probably a case where there is little profit margine.  The dealer may or may not make recommendations.  They have to think of their mechanics who probably detest working on a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||08-12-2000||12:28 PM||||Basically Fiero has the same sources of fires as all other cars on the road with 2 major additions.<P>1. The 84 and maybe some 85's had a batch of weak rods. Dealers didn't usually rebuild these motors but replaced them. The only way to know if you have your original motor is to find the serial number on the engine block and check it with GM.<P>2. All 4 cylinders have weak head bolts from the factory. These can break allowing collant and/or oil to run onto the exhaust. (Coolant Will burn under some conditions) This is why I keep telling people Do Not Ever reuse 4 cyl head bolts.<P>A leaking valve cover gasket can allow oil to run onto the head and exhaust. That's easy to fix. Even if the car didn't have problems with this gasket to start with it likely does now. The make sure the new valve cover gasket comes with a EGR gasket. You have to take off the EGR valve to replace the valve cover gasket. (There is a valve cover bolt under the EGR) The number one thing people do wrong with this repair is over tighten the valve cover bolts. The number 2 thing is not getting things clean. Clean sealing surfaces on the head and cover with brake cleaner.<P>Another thing to watch out for is the exhaust and catalyst. Fiero's catalyst placement tends to be open to collecting crap both from things falling off the deck lid and things driven over. Always make sure things driven over exit the back. IF they don't you should probly pull over and make sure thay aren't on the catalyst. This is true of all cars but Fiero is a little more prone to it because the catalyst is mounted with a larger cross section facing forward.<P>The rest is common sense. keep things clean. Any motor covered in crap or leaking is likely to catch fire.<BR>||||reg||1
